# Fleettick operator runbook — 600s fleet automation (RFC-0013)

File: openOODA/docs/fleettick-runbook.oot

The fleettick binary performs ONE tick and exits. Cadence is supplied by
an external scheduler — a systemd user timer (preferred) or cron. There
is no in-process loop and no daemon. Overlap safety is a timestamp lock
at `.fleet-tick/lock`; a fresh lock makes a second invocation print
`TICK SKIP` and exit 0.

## 1. Build and install the binary

From the repo root, with the gate env exported (see
oodac/docs/runtime-env.oot — the env is a runtime contract):

  oodac.real build -o /tmp/fleettick buildbox/fleettick/sweep_main.oo
  install -m 0755 /tmp/fleettick "$HOME/.local/bin/fleettick"

Smoke it once by hand before scheduling:

  OODA_NO_JAIL=1 OO_LIST_AMBIENT_QUOTA=8589934592 \
  OODA_FLEET_HOME="$HOME" OODA_FLEET_XDG="$XDG_RUNTIME_DIR" \
  "$HOME/.local/bin/fleettick" /Projects/openOODA

Expect `TICK_LOCK took`, all 12 sections, `TICK done`, exit 0.

## 2. Start / stop (systemd user timer — preferred)

Units ship inert under `buildbox/fleettick/deploy/`. Install:

  install -D -m 0644 buildbox/fleettick/deploy/fleettick.service \
    "$HOME/.config/systemd/user/fleettick.service"
  install -D -m 0644 buildbox/fleettick/deploy/fleettick.timer \
    "$HOME/.config/systemd/user/fleettick.timer"
  systemctl --user daemon-reload

Enable (operator action — units are shipped disabled):

  systemctl --user enable --now fleettick.timer
  systemctl --user list-timers fleettick.timer   # confirm NEXT ~600s

Stop the cadence, keep the unit:

  systemctl --user stop fleettick.timer          # timer off, state kept
  systemctl --user disable fleettick.timer       # no restart at login

Run one extra tick by hand (safe — lock arbitrates):

  systemctl --user start fleettick.service

## 3. Start / stop (cron — fallback)

Append `buildbox/fleettick/deploy/fleettick.cron` to `crontab -e`.
Stop: remove the line. The fragment derives OODA_FLEET_XDG itself —
cron supplies no XDG_RUNTIME_DIR.

## 4. Read state

  cat /Projects/openOODA/.fleet-state.txt    # last tick's full verdicts
  ls  /Projects/openOODA/.fleet-alerts/      # one file per live breach
  journalctl --user -u fleettick.service --since "1 hour ago"
  tail  "$HOME/.local/state/fleettick.log"   # cron path

Healthy sign-off line pattern: `TICK_LOCK took` … `TICK_UNLOCK`,
`TICK done`, `alerts=0`. `TICK_LOCK held age=N` + `TICK SKIP` means a
previous tick was still running — normal, not a failure.

Alert kinds in the spool: ALERT-cutoff|drift|down|blocked|warn|err-*.
They auto-clear when the condition recovers (ALERT_CLEAR in the log).

## 5. Recover the lock

Lock file: `/Projects/openOODA/.fleet-tick/lock` (one timestamp line).

- Fresh (<1200 s): a tick is running or just finished — do nothing.
- Stale (>1200 s): the next tick steals it automatically
  (`TICK_LOCK stole age=N`). No manual action needed.
- Manual force (only if you must run NOW and the holder is dead):

      rm /Projects/openOODA/.fleet-tick/lock

- A crashed tick leaves the lock; the stale-steal path covers it on
  the next scheduled run — worst case one skipped cadence.
- If `TICK_LOCK_ERR` or `TICK_UNLOCK_ERR` appears, check filesystem
  writable perms on `.fleet-tick/` and free space on that volume.

## 6. Failure modes → meaning

| Line | Meaning | Action |
|------|---------|--------|
| `FORGE_ERR …` | github ls-remote failed | check network; gate closes, all repos GATE-SKIP — safe |
| `HEALTH proc herdr DOWN` | herdr not running | restart herdr; tick continues |
| `RECON … DIRTY-SKIP` | repo has uncommitted work | expected; reconcile won't touch it |
| `RECON … DIVERGED` | local/remote both moved | manual merge — tick never forces |
| `AGENTS_ERR` | herdr socket unreachable | check OODA_FLEET_HOME/XDG env |
| `QUOTA <a> CUTOFF` | agent ≥95% used | pace work off that agent |

## 7. Known limits (from soak 2026-11-06)

- Pane-quota `UNKNOWN` is normal when no usage footer is visible.
- `PULLED`/`DIVERGED`/`PULL_ERR` not yet exercised live (all repos SYNC).
- A real alert firing has not been observed in soak — spool logic is
  unit-tested but watch the first genuine breach.
